    <section class="limits" id="limits">
      <header class="shead">
        <p class="eyebrow">before you download it</p>
        <h2>When Topics is the wrong tool.</h2>
        <p class="shead__lead">Written by the people who make it, which is the only reason it is worth reading.</p>
      </header>
      {/* SIX VERDICTS, NOT SIX PARAGRAPHS. What was here was six h3+p cards in a
           grid: honest, and a wall. A disqualification list only does its job if
           you can SCAN it and find yourself in it, so each row now states the
           situation in the reader's own voice, gives the verdict as a word, and
           then says where to go instead. Naming the alternative is the part that
           makes it worth reading — a page that only apologises is still selling. */}
      <ul class="verdicts">
        <li class="verdict">
          <p class="verdict__if">You run one agent, watch it, and finish.</p>
          <p class="verdict__tag" data-v="wrong">Wrong tool</p>
          <p class="verdict__why">Then a workspace is overhead and the terminal you already have is better. Everything here is built for the case where three or four are running and you have lost track of which one is waiting on you.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="verdict">
          <p class="verdict__if">You want the work to carry on after you shut the lid.</p>
          <p class="verdict__tag" data-v="wrong">Wrong tool</p>
          <p class="verdict__why">It will not. The agents are processes on your machine and they stop when it sleeps. If that is the requirement you want cloud workspaces, and the <a href="/compare/claude-code-guis">comparison page</a> says which products have them.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="verdict">
          <p class="verdict__if">You need accounts, roles or a board your team shares.</p>
          <p class="verdict__tag" data-v="wrong">Wrong tool</p>
          <p class="verdict__why">There are no accounts and no team roles, and no sign-up anywhere. Devices pair instead: the machine Topics runs on is the owner, any other device is approved once from it, and the token it gets can be revoked from the same panel. Boards sync between <em>your</em> machines, which is a different thing from a board your team shares.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="verdict">
          <p class="verdict__if">You were hoping it would replace your editor.</p>
          <p class="verdict__tag" data-v="design">By design</p>
          <p class="verdict__why">Topics is not an IDE and is not becoming one. It hosts the terminal agents and the surfaces around them, and it expects you to keep whatever editor you already use, open beside it.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="verdict">
          <p class="verdict__if">macOS will tell you the developer cannot be verified.</p>
          <p class="verdict__tag" data-v="cost">A real cost</p>
          <p class="verdict__why">The build is signed ad-hoc rather than with a paid Apple certificate, so Gatekeeper stops the first launch, and since Sequoia the control-click bypass is gone. <a href="#download">Three steps in Settings</a>, about fifteen seconds, once. If that is a dealbreaker it is a fair one.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="verdict">
          <p class="verdict__if">You are on Windows or Linux.</p>
          <p class="verdict__tag" data-v="young">Not yet</p>
          <p class="verdict__why">{stats.since !== null && `First commit ${longDate(stats.since)}. `}Both are built and released on every tag and both work, and both are used far less than macOS, which is exactly where you should expect to find the rough edges first.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      {/* Anyone convinced by this section had 3,000px to scroll before finding a
           button on the live page. */}
