    {/* ── THE LONG TAIL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Sixteen tiles, each an icon and one sentence in a 282×125 box, average
         84 characters. Which is to say: sixteen feature NAMES, arranged. A
         reader could not tell from any of them what the thing does, only that it
         exists, and a grid of things that exist is a spec sheet.

         So it is told instead. Six that have a mechanism worth a paragraph get
         one, with the evidence beside them — the actual flag, the actual
         keystroke, the actual file that gets written — and the rest are a
         sentence at the bottom, in prose, which is what a genuinely minor
         feature deserves. No icons: a 17px glyph beside "Checkpoints" was
         decoration standing in for an explanation. */}
    <section class="more" id="more">
      <header class="shead">
        <p class="eyebrow">also in the box</p>
        <h2>Six more, told properly.</h2>
        <p class="shead__lead">All shipped, all free. None of them is the reason to install Topics, and each is a reason to keep it.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="tail">
        <article class="tail__row">
          <div class="tail__say">
            <h3>Checkpoints roll back the working tree, not just the chat</h3>
            <p>
              Every coding assistant lets you delete messages. That undoes the conversation and
              leaves the files exactly as the agent left them, which is the half that matters.
              A checkpoint in Topics is a marker on the conversation <em>and</em> a snapshot of the
              worktree: return to it and the branch goes back with you, staged changes included.
              It is how you let an agent try something expensive without a decision to make first.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tail__ev">
            <p class="tail__evh">what a restore touches</p>
            <ul class="tail__list">
              <li><span>conversation</span><b>truncated to the mark</b></li>
              <li><span>worktree</span><b>restored, staged and all</b></li>
              <li><span>branch head</span><b>moved back</b></li>
              <li><span>scrollback</span><b>kept, so you can read why</b></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="tail__row">
          <div class="tail__say">
            <h3>A task cannot ask for review until your checks pass</h3>
            <p>
              Per board, you name the commands that have to succeed inside the worktree before an
              agent is allowed to move a card to review. They run where the work is, not on your
              checkout, so a failing test blocks that one task and nothing else. An agent that
              cannot get them green comes back and says so instead of handing you a red branch
              and a paragraph about how it should work.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tail__ev">
            <p class="tail__evh">a board's gate</p>
            <pre class="tail__code"><code>bun run typecheck
bun test tests/unit
bun run check:lint</code></pre>
            <p class="tail__note">Run in the task's own checkout, before <b>review</b> is offered.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="tail__row">
          <div class="tail__say">
            <h3>A goal survives the compaction that eats your first message</h3>
            <p>
              Long sessions compact, and when they do the instruction you gave at the top is the
              first thing summarised into vagueness, which is why an agent forty turns in starts
              solving a slightly different problem. A topic goal is re-sent with every turn, so it
              is never the oldest thing in the window. Set it once per topic; it is the standing
              answer to "what are we actually doing".
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tail__ev">
            <p class="tail__evh">set for this topic</p>
            <p class="tail__quote">Port the sync layer to the new schema. Do not change the public API, and keep every existing test green.</p>
            <p class="tail__note">Re-sent every turn · survives <span class="mono">/compact</span></p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="tail__row">
          <div class="tail__say">
            <h3>Speak to it, and transcription never leaves the machine</h3>
            <p>
              Hold the key, talk, let go. The audio goes to a local Whisper build, not to us and not
              to a transcription API, and the text lands in the composer for you to edit before
              it is sent. Answers can be read back the same way. It is the difference between
              dictating a paragraph of context and typing it, on the days you would have typed a
              worse one.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tail__ev">
            <p class="tail__evh">where the audio goes</p>
            <ul class="tail__list">
              <li><span>capture</span><b>your machine</b></li>
              <li><span>transcription</span><b>whisper.cpp, on device</b></li>
              <li><span>network</span><b>nothing sent</b></li>
              <li><span>result</span><b>editable, before it sends</b></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="tail__row">
          <div class="tail__say">
            <h3>Every pane has an address you can paste to anyone</h3>
            <p>
              Copy a link to a tab and it names that exact pane: this terminal, in this group, in
              this project. Open it on the laptop and the laptop focuses it; open it on the phone
              over your tunnel and the phone does. Nothing is uploaded to make that work: the link
              is a locator, and both machines already sync their layout to each other.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tail__ev">
            <p class="tail__evh">the link is the locator</p>
            <pre class="tail__code"><code>topics://project/acme-web
  /group/principale
  /pane/terminal:cc1</code></pre>
            <p class="tail__note">Resolves on any device signed into the same sync.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="tail__row">
          <div class="tail__say">
            <h3>Work that starts without you being there</h3>
            <p>
              A schedule, or an HTTP call from anything that can make one. The nightly runs at
              three, the webhook fires when CI goes red, and the agent picks the task up in a
              worktree the way it would if you had written the card yourself. You wake up to a
              branch and a diff to read, or to nothing, which is also an answer.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tail__ev">
            <p class="tail__evh">two ways in</p>
            <ul class="tail__list">
              <li><span>cron</span><b>0 3 * * 1-5 · nightly triage</b></li>
              <li><span>webhook</span><b>POST /hooks/&lt;token&gt;</b></li>
              <li><span>lands as</span><b>a card on a board</b></li>
              <li><span>runs in</span><b>its own worktree</b></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      {/* The genuinely small ones. A sentence, in prose, because that is what
           they are worth — and putting them in cards was the thing that made
           sixteen features read as sixteen equals. */}
      <p class="tail__rest">
        Also, without ceremony: <b>auto-merge</b> per board for approved tasks that do not conflict ·
        <b>slash commands</b>, yours and the project's, with <code>@</code> for files and topics ·
        <b>a model per task</b>, so the cheap ones stay cheap · <b>sub-agents on screen</b> when one
        agent spawns a fleet · <b>typed questions</b>, a real form instead of guessing an agent's
        syntax · <b>conversation branches</b> from any earlier message · <b>full effort</b> passed on
        every spawn, so a session opens at the top tier · <b>a command palette</b> that will reopen
        the tab you just closed · and <b>two languages</b>, English and Italian.
      </p>
    </section>

